The sum of effectively given neighbourhood systems is effectively given, and the sum combinators are computable.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`sumEnum`).** The sum enumeration uses tag zero for left neighbourhoods, tag one for right neighbourhoods, and every other tag for the master neighbourhood.

**Theorem (`sumEnum_zero`).** An index tagged by zero denotes the corresponding left neighbourhood.

*Proof.* The outer tag test takes its zero branch, which returns the left injection at the second component of the index. ∎

**Theorem (`sumEnum_one`).** An index tagged by one denotes the corresponding right neighbourhood.

*Proof.* The zero branch is excluded and the next tag test takes its one branch, returning the right injection at the second component. ∎

**Theorem (`inj₀_eq_iff`).** Two left-injected neighbourhoods are equal exactly when their underlying left neighbourhoods are equal.

*Proof.* Injectivity of the corresponding tagged embedding gives the forward implication, and substitution gives the converse. ∎

**Theorem (`inj₀_eq_inj₁_elim`).** A nonempty left-injected neighbourhood cannot equal a right-injected neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Choose an element of the assumed nonempty summand; its left and right tags cannot coincide, contradicting the proposed equality. ∎

**Theorem (`inj₀_ne_inj₁_of_nonempty`).** A left-injected neighbourhood cannot equal a nonempty right-injected neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Choose an element of the assumed nonempty summand; its left and right tags cannot coincide, contradicting the proposed equality. ∎

**Theorem (`inj₀_ne_sumMaster`).** No left-injected neighbourhood equals the master neighbourhood of the sum.

*Proof.* The sum master contains the distinguished untagged point, whereas an injected summand contains only tagged points. ∎

**Theorem (`inj₁_eq_iff`).** Two right-injected neighbourhoods are equal exactly when their underlying right neighbourhoods are equal.

*Proof.* Injectivity of the corresponding tagged embedding gives the forward implication, and substitution gives the converse. ∎

**Theorem (`inj₁_ne_sumMaster`).** No right-injected neighbourhood equals the master neighbourhood of the sum.

*Proof.* The sum master contains the distinguished untagged point, whereas an injected summand contains only tagged points. ∎

**Theorem (`recDec_setEq₀`).** Equality of left neighbourhoods whose indices are computed primitively is recursively decidable.

*Proof.* Set equality is mutual inclusion, so compose the presentation’s decidable inclusion relation with the two primitive-recursive index functions in both orders and conjoin the results. ∎

**Theorem (`recDec_setEq₁`).** Equality of right neighbourhoods whose indices are computed primitively is recursively decidable.

*Proof.* Set equality is mutual inclusion, so compose the presentation’s decidable inclusion relation with the two primitive-recursive index functions in both orders and conjoin the results. ∎

**Theorem (`sumEnum_master`).** Every index tagged by a number other than zero or one denotes the master neighbourhood of the sum.

*Proof.* Both distinguished tag tests fail, so the remaining branch is the sum master neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`sumEnum_mem`).** Every set produced by the tagged sum enumeration is a sum neighbourhood.

*Proof.* The three tag cases are respectively a valid left neighbourhood, a valid right neighbourhood, and the master neighbourhood, all admitted by the definition of the sum system. ∎

**Theorem (`sumEnum_nonempty`).** Every neighbourhood produced by the tagged sum enumeration is nonempty.

*Proof.* Inspect the tag: a left or right neighbourhood is nonempty by the corresponding factor hypothesis, while the master case contains the distinguished untagged point. ∎

**Theorem (`sumEnum_subset_sumMaster`).** Every neighbourhood in the sum enumeration is contained in the master neighbourhood.

*Proof.* In each tag case the injected factor neighbourhood lies in the sum master; the remaining tag already denotes the master itself. ∎

**Theorem (`sumEnum_inter_sumMaster`).** Intersecting an enumerated sum neighbourhood with the master neighbourhood leaves it unchanged.

*Proof.* Every enumerated sum neighbourhood is contained in the master neighbourhood, so the left-hand absorption law for intersection applies. ∎

**Theorem (`sumMaster_inter_sumEnum`).** Intersecting the master neighbourhood with an enumerated sum neighbourhood leaves that neighbourhood unchanged.

*Proof.* Every enumerated sum neighbourhood lies inside the master neighbourhood, so the right-hand absorption law for intersection applies. ∎

**Theorem (`tag_trichotomy`).** Every natural-number tag is zero, one, or distinct from both zero and one.

*Proof.* Decidable equality first separates zero; in the remaining case it separates one, leaving a tag unequal to both. ∎

**Theorem (`sumEnum_eq_iff`).** Two tagged sum indices denote the same neighbourhood exactly when both are master tags, or both have the same summand tag and equal underlying factor neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* The nine combinations of left, right, and master tags are disjoint: equal tags reduce by injectivity to equality in one factor, and two master tags are automatically equal. ∎

**Theorem (`eqSEdec`).** Equality between two neighbourhoods in the tagged sum enumeration is recursively decidable.

*Proof.* Decide the tags of both indices.  Master tags agree with one another, matching left or right tags invoke decidable equality in that factor, and all mixed tags are unequal. ∎

**Definition (`sumPresentation`).** The tagged enumeration of left, right, and master neighbourhoods forms a computable presentation of the sum.

**Theorem (`sum_isEffectivelyGiven`).** The sum neighbourhood system is effectively given.

*Proof.* Choose computable presentations of the two factors; their tagged sum presentation supplies a computable presentation of the sum. ∎

**Theorem (`sumPresentation_X`).** The neighbourhood family of the sum presentation is exactly the tagged sum enumeration.

*Proof.* This is the corresponding defining equation after substituting the displayed arguments. ∎

**Theorem (`inMap₀_isComputable`).** The left injection into an effectively presented sum is computable.

*Proof.* Split the target index into left, right and master tags.  The left case is exactly decidable inclusion in the first factor, the right case is impossible by nonemptiness, and the master case always holds. ∎

**Theorem (`inMap₁_isComputable`).** The right injection into an effectively presented sum is computable.

*Proof.* Split the target index into left, right and master tags.  The right case is exactly decidable inclusion in the second factor, the left case is impossible by nonemptiness, and the master case always holds. ∎

**Theorem (`outMap₀_isComputable`).** The map extracting the left component of an effectively presented sum is computable.

*Proof.* Choose an index for the first master neighbourhood.  A left-tagged source reduces to factor inclusion, while right-tagged and master sources both reduce to inclusion of that chosen master index. ∎

**Theorem (`outMap₁_isComputable`).** The map extracting the right component of an effectively presented sum is computable.

*Proof.* Choose an index for the second master neighbourhood.  A right-tagged source reduces to factor inclusion, while left-tagged and master sources both reduce to inclusion of that chosen master index. ∎

**Theorem (`sumMap_isComputable`).** The map induced componentwise by two computable maps between sums is computable.

*Proof.* The target master case is automatic.  Matching left tags use the recursively enumerable relation for the first component map, matching right tags use that for the second, and all mismatched nonmaster tags are excluded by disjointness and nonemptiness. ∎
